<h1>2.5 CDNs, proxies and static files</h1>
<p class="lead">Between the browser and your server there can be shared caches that serve thousands of users from one copy. The same <code>Cache-Control</code> header controls them too.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="2.5"></div>


<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>Private caches and shared caches</h2>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>Private cache</b>
        <p class="muted">Belongs to one user: the browser cache. It may store anything not marked <code>no-store</code>, including <code>private</code> responses.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <b>Shared cache</b>
        <p class="muted">Serves many users: CDNs, reverse proxies (Nginx, Varnish), company proxies, and the response cache from lesson 2.3. It must never store <code>private</code> responses.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>What a CDN does</h2>
<p>A <b>Content Delivery Network</b> (Cloudflare, Amazon CloudFront, Azure Front Door, Fastly, Akamai...) has servers called <b>edge locations</b> in hundreds of cities. Users connect to the nearest edge. If the edge has a fresh copy, it answers in a few milliseconds. If not, it asks your server (the <b>origin</b>), stores the answer and serves the next users from it.</p>
<div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>User in Tokyo</b><small>Requests <code>/css/site.css</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step hit"><b>Nearest edge</b><small>HIT: answers in ~10 ms</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Only on a MISS</b><small>Edge asks the origin</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step miss"><b>Origin server</b><small>Your app (for example on Render)</small></div>
</div>
<p>Benefits: much lower latency for users far from your server, far less traffic and load on the origin, and protection against traffic spikes because the edge absorbs them.</p>

<h2>Headers that matter for shared caches</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>public</code> / <code>private</code></td><td>Whether a shared cache may store the response at all.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>s-maxage=600</code></td><td>Lifetime for shared caches only. A common pattern is <code>max-age=60, s-maxage=600</code>: browsers check every minute, the CDN keeps it 10 minutes. You can purge the CDN, but not users' browsers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Vary</code></td><td>Lists request headers that change the response, for example <code>Vary: Accept-Encoding</code> (gzip or brotli). The cache keeps one copy per value. <code>Vary: User-Agent</code> creates thousands of copies and kills the hit ratio. <code>Vary: *</code> means never reuse.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Age</code></td><td>Added by the cache: how many seconds ago the copy was fetched from the origin.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CF-Cache-Status</code>, <code>X-Cache</code></td><td>Vendor headers that tell you whether the CDN answered with a HIT, MISS, EXPIRED, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>stale-if-error</code></td><td>Lets the CDN keep serving content if your origin is down.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>The cache key and purging</h2>
<p>A CDN identifies a cached object by its <b>cache key</b>, usually the host, path and query string. <code>/style.css?v=1</code> and <code>/style.css?v=2</code> are different objects. To update content before its TTL ends you <b>purge</b> it (remove it from all edges through the CDN's dashboard or API, by URL, prefix or tag). Purging takes seconds to minutes and does not reach browsers that already have a fresh copy.</p>

<h2>Static files: versioning instead of purging</h2>
<p>CSS, JavaScript and images are perfect for caching, but you also want users to get changes immediately after a deploy. The standard solution is <b>cache busting</b> (fingerprinting): put a version in the URL that changes whenever the file changes, and cache that URL forever.</p>
<p>This portal does it already. In the layout:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/site.css" asp-append-version="true" /&gt;</code></pre>
<p>ASP.NET Core renders it as <code>/css/site.css?v=&lt;hash of the file&gt;</code>. When you edit the file, the hash changes, so the URL is new and every cache fetches it fresh. In <code>Program.cs</code> the static file middleware then sets:</p>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Cache-Control</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>/css/site.css?v=...</code></td><td><code>public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code></td><td>This URL's content can never change, so cache it for a year and never revalidate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/css/site.css</code></td><td><code>no-cache</code></td><td>Without a version the content can change, so always revalidate. The static file middleware sends an ETag automatically, so it is usually a cheap 304.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

</details>

