<div class="callout">
    <strong>About the live site on Render</strong>
    Render runs your app as the origin. It does not cache your API responses for you. To cache at the edge you would put a CDN such as Cloudflare in front of your domain. It would then follow the <code>public</code>, <code>s-maxage</code> and <code>Vary</code> headers described above.
</div>

<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Versioned file.</b> Inspect the <em>site.css with version</em> preset. You see <code>immutable</code> and a one-year <code>max-age</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Unversioned file.</b> Inspect <em>site.css without version</em>. You see <code>no-cache</code> plus an <code>ETag</code> and <code>Last-Modified</code>, so browsers revalidate every time.</li>
    <li><b>Compare the API endpoints.</b> Inspect the endpoints from lessons 2.1 to 2.4. Notice that the output cache endpoint sends no <code>Cache-Control</code> at all: output caching is invisible to browsers and CDNs.</li>
    <li><b>Look in DevTools.</b> Reload this page with DevTools &rarr; Network open. The versioned CSS and JS files show "(memory cache)" or "(disk cache)": the browser did not contact the server for them.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Shared caches (CDNs, proxies) serve one stored copy to many users, controlled by <code>public</code>, <code>s-maxage</code> and <code>Vary</code>. Never let them store personal data: use <code>private</code> or <code>no-store</code>. For static files, put a version in the URL and cache it forever with <code>immutable</code>. A new version means a new URL, so you never need to purge.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    A page with a user's name cached as <code>public</code> at the CDN, so every visitor sees the first user's name. This has happened to real companies. Other mistakes: long <code>max-age</code> on unversioned files, so users keep old CSS after a deploy, and <code>Vary</code> on headers with many values.
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Level 2 complete</strong>
    You can now control browser caching with <code>Cache-Control</code>, save bandwidth with ETags and 304s, cache whole responses on the server with response caching and output caching, and reason about CDNs and static files. Level 3 returns to application code and compares the main caching strategies: cache-aside, read-through, write-through, write-behind, write-around and refresh-ahead.
</div>

